hello my name is betsy espinal and this  is my final project  for genomics in the virtual lab and it  is titled  viruses as a cystic fibrosis cure and  this project  is building off of my last project that  i did which is just on cystic fibrosis  an introduction to cystic fibrosis  cystic fibrosis is a serious disease  that affects the lungs and digestive  system causing pancreatic insufficiency  and recurrent respiratory infections  it results from a deletion type mutation  in the cftr gene  this disease is most common in caucasian  populations and is autosomal recessive  there is currently no cure for cystic  fibrosis treatments include medicines to  help control symptoms prevent  complications and make the condition  easier to live with  prenatal screening is also an option for  parents of unborn children  this is the healthy cftr gene  the pdf code is 5uak  and it is pictured on the left in a  person without cystic fibrosis the  cystic fibrosis transmembrane regulator  protein functions  as a chloride ion channel that regulates  fluid in airways  and other regions the cftr gene is  located on chromosome 7.  as we can see on the left whenever the  protein is phosphorylated the channel  opens and allows  ions to pass through and this allows the  cell to regulate fluids  in the lungs and this is unhealthy cftr  gene which leads to an unhealthy cftr  protein  and the pdf code for this is  4wz6 most common mutation that leads to  cystic fibrosis  is called delta f508 this mutation  involves the deletion of three  nucleotides and causes 70  of all cystic fibrosis cases and on the  top left  i have a model of the two  cftr proteins on top of each other and  this region is just the regulatory  domain because that's where the  mutations occur  and the red parts are the mutations the  cftr protein in cystic fibrosis cannot  properly manage fluid regulation due to  its structure  in the regulatory domain which leads to  the channel not being able to open  when the regulatory domain is  phosphorylated so basically what happens  is the phosphate activates the  regulatory domain  that tells the channel to open but  in people with cystic fibrosis this  doesn't happen because of mutations that  happen in the regulatory domain and this  leads to  lots of problems for someone diagnosed  with cystic fibrosis dealing with this  disease can be extremely  emotionally distressing patients face  low life expectancy  the life expectancy is only 37 years  chronic symptoms and decreasing bodily  function that causes disability  as patients enter adulthood their  emotional well-being is known to  decrease as their condition becomes more  severe  because of the disease's specific nature  there has been no success curing it  so what is the solution for a disease  this serious it is very clear that  treating the symptoms has its limits  it is necessary to tackle the issue at  the genetic level using gene therapy  what is gene therapy and could it cure  cystic fibrosis  gene therapy introduces healthy genetic  material into cells in order to correct  abnormalities that cause disease  vectors are the carriers of this genetic  material they're usually modified  viruses  so in this diagram it shows a virus  carrying the cftr  gene it would infect the person through  the respiratory tract  enter the lungs infect the cells with  the protein  and deliver the therapeutic genes that  would in theory cure the person  cystic fibrosis was one of the first  targets of gene therapy  when the practice picked up viability  two decades ago  however there has been no lasting  success so far targeting the lungs is  particularly tricky because our lungs  are evolved to be extremely virus  resistant  even though our lungs are this way we  know that viruses like coronaviruses  influenza adenoviruses lentiviruses  and others do succeed in causing  respiratory illness  could we use one of these as a vector  and cure cystic fibrosis  so obviously my answer to that would be  yes we probably could  and out of the viruses listed in the  last  slide adenoviruses and lentiviruses are  the most promising  and here's why so both of them do really  well  in terms of the four main categories  that are considered  when looking at possible vectors and  these are duration of expression  targeted cells packaging capacity and  immunogenic potential  for adenovirus vectors it is  transient which means it doesn't  integrate into the genome  and for the lentivirus it it does  integrate into the genome which makes it  in theory a permanent solution while the  adenovirus would need to be  re just reapplied targeted cells both of  them  are respiratory and widespread meaning  they could  infect the respiratory cells and also  other cells  in the body packaging capacity this is  how much  space the vector has to hold therapeutic  gene material  and for the adenovirus it is 7.5  kb which stands for kilobases and for  the lentivirus  it is 9.7 kilobases the immunogenic  potential  means how likely is it to trigger a  immune response like anaphylaxis  in the patient that is given to and  obviously we don't want that to happen  because that's very dangerous  and the adenovirus factor it's  considered pretty high  and for the lentivirus it's considered  relatively low  so even though both candidates are  promising lentivirus is the most  promising  due to its long-term expression slightly  larger packaging potential  and low probability of immune response  but putting it together  the healthy cftr gene is approximately  189  kb in length which greatly exceeds the  9.7 kb packaging capacity  luckily studies suggest that only 5 to  10 percent of normal cfdr gene  is required to reverse disease effect  and this is because only this region of  the protein  is not functioning the rest of it is  okay and we don't need to  use precious space in the packaging  capacity  to store this information when it's  already okay  we need this information and that only  accounts for  five to ten percent um so the mutated  cftr gene is in the section that codes  for the regulatory domain  which is called ndb1 and is located on  exon 9. so this region is the region we  want to take that  5 to 10 percent because this region  codes for this region which is the  problem we only have  9.7 kb from exon 9  and that accounts for 5.15  of the normal cftr gene which does fall  into the range  of what is considered  enough to reverse the disease effect so  yes  the lentivirus does have enough space  and it might be able to hold all the  information needed to reverse the  disease's effect  so summary cystic fibrosis is a serious  disease of the transmembrane proteins  involved in chloride ion transport it is  currently incurable  and that's really why i wanted to look  into cures or possible cures for  my final project healthy cftr proteins  are able to open  after the nbd1 region of the protein is  phosphorylated but in people with the  delta  f508 mutation this region is deformed  and unable to open using gene therapy  this illness  may be able to be cured the ideal viral  vector for the delivery of the  therapeutic gene would be a modified  lentivirus because of its duration of  expression  packaging capacity and low immunogenic  potential  modifying a lentivirus and packaging it  with genetic material from  exon 9 might be able to cure cystic  fibrosis and there's my project  so what i learned is all about  viral vectors because i knew about them  and i knew about their use  in gene therapy but i didn't really know  why we chose the viruses  that scientists choose and why they're  able to  be effective it's really interesting to  research  all about that and also there are  promising studies  using the lentivirus in humans to cure  cystic fibrosis but up until now  there hasn't been a total cure but it is  looking  like lunch virus might be the way to go  and now that i've done my own research  into why that is and why it makes sense  i understand here  is my links for raw data and  reference publications and that was my  presentation thank you for watching
 

